    "title": "Wylie South Elementary prepares to welcome first students - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Kira Jinkins ,\n5 hours ago\nABILENE, Texas ( KTAB/KRBC ) — While students across much of the Big Country enjoy their final days of summer, teachers at Wylie South Elementary have been spending weeks unpacking boxes, hanging decorations and putting the finishing touches on the district’s newest campus.\nStorm shelter at new Wylie ISD campus to enhance student safety When the school opens its doors next week, students will be welcomed into a brand-new building featuring new classrooms, a library, cafeteria and updated technology. But inside those classrooms, it’s the teachers’ personalities that stand out.\nFor first-year kindergarten teacher Amberly Patterson, the opportunity to begin her teaching career in a brand-new school feels special.\n“It’s a surreal feeling, I can’t even put it into words,” Patterson said. “Being a first-year teacher and knowing that I have students who are coming in for their first year of school, all while in a brand-new school… it’s such a blessing and an answered prayer.”\nPatterson completed her student teaching at Wylie before interviewing for what she initially considered to be a practice interview. She said she quickly realized Wylie was where she was meant to be.\n“I really felt called,” Patterson said. “It was not my plan. I was planning to go out to the Metroplex and experience the city life. Then I sat down for the Wylie interview and felt this calling. I knew I needed to be here.”\nHer goal for the school year centers on one word: resilient.\n“I want them coming in every day knowing that this is a safe space for them, that they are loved and welcome,” Patterson said. “I want to challenge them to grow.”\nWylie ISD superintendent looks ahead to retirement, district future Down the hall, fourth-grade reading and writing teacher Peyton Dill is beginning her fifth year in the classroom with a theme inspired by a major life event.\nAfter marrying her husband last year, Dill adopted her husband’s last name and made it her classroom theme.\n“My husband and I met playing pickleball,” Dill said. “So, my room theme for this year is pickleball with lots of pickles around the room.”\nThe classroom is filled with pickleball paddles, pickle decorations and even references to her dog, Pickles.\nDill credits much of the room’s design to her mom.\n“I really have to give it to my mom,” Dill said. “My mom is a big decorator, so she definitely has the eye for things. I have the vision, and then she helps me put it where it needs to go.”\nNow that the decorating is finished, Dill said she’s ready for the students to arrive.\n“I’m excited to get to know my new students,” she said. “They are all so unique and so funny, and that’s probably the best part about teaching, just becoming friends with them as well and getting to know who they are at 10 and 11 years old.”\nFrom Abilene to the World Stage: Students reflect on global championship win",
    "summary": "ABILENE, Texas ( KTAB/KRBC ) — While students across much of the Big Country enjoy their final days of summer, teachers at Wylie South Elementary have been spending weeks unpacking boxes, hanging decorations and putting the finishing touches on the district’s newest campus. For first-year kindergarten teacher Amberly Patterson, the opportunity to begin her teaching career in a brand-new school feels special.",
